+- [S3] $N_{depth}$: Number of iterations for supervised training on unmasked pixels before applying depth loss.
 
 ## Background
-The integration of monocular depth priors into NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the limitations of sparse-view reconstruction, where photometric consistency alone is insufficient to resolve depth ambiguities. Early works like NerfingMVS (September 2021) utilized COLMAP-extracted sparse point clouds and fine-tuned monocular depth networks to guide volume sampling [S3]. Concurrently, methods like DS-NeRF explored depth completion networks for RGB-D data [S1]. The DaRF framework (2023) introduced joint optimization of NeRF and MDE to enforce multi-view consistency [S2, unofficial]. More recent approaches have explored distributional matching via Earth Mover’s Distance [S1] and the use of shadow rays for single-view reconstruction [S1]. These methods collectively address the challenges of scale ambiguity, local geometric detail preservation, and robustness to noisy priors in few-shot NeRF settings.
+The integration of monocular depth priors into NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the limitations of sparse-view reconstruction, where photometric loss alone leads to shape-radiance ambiguity and poor geometry [S2, unofficial]. Early works like NerfingMVS (September 2021) utilized COLMAP sparse point clouds and pre-trained monocular depth networks to guide volume sampling [S3]. Subsequent developments, such as DäRF (2023), introduced joint optimization of the NeRF and the MDE network to enforce multi-view consistency and adapt the MDE to the specific scene geometry [S2, unofficial]. More recent approaches leverage diffusion-based depth estimators and Earth Mover’s Distance to supervise the distribution of ray termination distances, further refining geometric accuracy in few-shot settings [S1].
 
